# Standard JSON parsing and building surface.
#
# Phase 33R5b — the executing JSON stdlib. Two complementary
# shapes:
#
#   1. Opaque JsonValue + typed accessors — for dynamic JSON
#      (LLM responses, debug tooling, polymorphic API
#      responses). Parse text into a JsonValue, then access
#      fields via typed getters that return Result<T, String>
#      so type mismatches and missing fields surface as
#      recoverable errors.
#
#   2. Typed-decoder convention (lands fully in 33R5b-b) — for
#      typed APIs where the JSON shape matches a declared
#      Corvid struct. User code declares a tool with the
#      target type as its return type; the runtime decodes
#      via serde + json_to_value against the declared type
#      table.
#
# JSON has no security boundary beyond serde's own validation
# (no I/O, no network, no filesystem). The structural property
# the surface carries is `json.parse_safety_no_panic`: malformed
# input returns Err, never panics.
#
# All three tools are non-deterministic from the typechecker's
# perspective (decl-replayability rejects tool calls inside
# @deterministic bodies regardless of effect), even though the
# operations themselves are pure and process-internal. Programs
# that need deterministic JSON access can wrap the calls in
# their own audit layer or factor parse calls into a separate,
# non-deterministic agent.

# The four JSON effect rows. Read effects are reversible
# (parsing and accessing JSON doesn't mutate observable state);
# the build path is also reversible since `json_object_finish`
# is a snapshot, not a commit. Named `json_egress_*` so the tool
# names don't collide with the effect names (effects and tools
# share a namespace at the resolver level — same trap as 33S1's
# io_*, 33S2's http_*, 33S3's db_egress_*).
effect json_egress_read:
    reversible: true

effect json_egress_build:
    reversible: true

public tool json_parse(text: String) -> Result<JsonValue, String> uses json_egress_read

# Typed accessors. Each one returns Result<T, String> so the
# typechecker forces user code to handle parse-time / access-
# time errors. The error message names the field that violated
# the expectation so user code can route a useful diagnostic
# up to its callers.
public tool json_get_int(value: JsonValue, field: String) -> Result<Int, String> uses json_egress_read
public tool json_get_float(value: JsonValue, field: String) -> Result<Float, String> uses json_egress_read
public tool json_get_string(value: JsonValue, field: String) -> Result<String, String> uses json_egress_read
public tool json_get_bool(value: JsonValue, field: String) -> Result<Bool, String> uses json_egress_read
public tool json_get_object(value: JsonValue, field: String) -> Result<JsonValue, String> uses json_egress_read
public tool json_get_array(value: JsonValue, field: String) -> Result<List<JsonValue>, String> uses json_egress_read

# Builder side. `json_object_new` returns an empty builder;
# `json_object_set_*` mutate the inner Mutex and return the
# SAME builder for fluent chaining (clones share access to the
# same underlying Map); `json_object_finish` snapshots the
# current state and serialises to a String. The builder remains
# usable for further set+finish cycles — the semantics are
# snapshot, not consume.
public tool json_object_new() -> JsonBuilder uses json_egress_build
public tool json_object_set_int(builder: JsonBuilder, key: String, value: Int) -> JsonBuilder uses json_egress_build
public tool json_object_set_float(builder: JsonBuilder, key: String, value: Float) -> JsonBuilder uses json_egress_build
public tool json_object_set_string(builder: JsonBuilder, key: String, value: String) -> JsonBuilder uses json_egress_build
public tool json_object_set_bool(builder: JsonBuilder, key: String, value: Bool) -> JsonBuilder uses json_egress_build
public tool json_object_finish(builder: JsonBuilder) -> String uses json_egress_build
